{
  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThis component is not partially accessible \u2014 it is entirely inaccessible to keyboard users and screen reader users. Every interactive element is a non-focusable `<div>` with a mouse-only `onClick` handler. There is zero ARIA, zero keyboard support, zero semantic structure, and zero state communication to assistive technology. This is not a \"design decision\" gap; it is a complete absence of accessibility. The component would fail WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard) at the most fundamental level: keyboard users cannot reach, activate, or navigate any element in this menu.\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore reading the code, I predicted for a mega menu component:\n1. **Predicted**: Divs used instead of `<nav>`/`<ul>`/`<button>` \u2014 **CONFIRMED** (lines 7, 8, 10, 17, 20)\n2. **Predicted**: No keyboard event handlers (Enter, Space, Escape, Arrow keys) \u2014 **CONFIRMED** (only `onClick` on line 13)\n3. **Predicted**: No `aria-expanded`, `aria-haspopup`, or `aria-controls` \u2014 **CONFIRMED** (zero ARIA attributes in entire component)\n4. **Predicted**: No focus management or restoration \u2014 **CONFIRMED** (no `ref`, no `focus()`, no focus logic)\n5. **Predicted**: No landmark structure \u2014 **CONFIRMED** (no `<nav>`, no `role=\"navigation\"`)\n\nAll five predictions confirmed. No surprises \u2014 the component is uniformly absent of accessibility.\n\n---\n\n## Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n### 1. Interactive elements are non-focusable `<div>` elements \u2014 keyboard users cannot access the menu at all\n\n**Evidence**: Lines 10\u201314. The menu trigger is a `<div>` with `onClick`. Divs are not natively focusable. There is no `tabindex`, no `onKeyDown`, no `role=\"button\"`.\n\n```jsx\n// Line 10-14\n<div\n  key={idx}\n  className=\"menu-item\"\n  onClick={() => setOpenMenu(openMenu === idx ? null : idx)}\n>\n```\n\n**User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users (complete access loss), screen reader users (no button role announced), motor-impairment users who rely on keyboard activation.\n\n**WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard) \u2014 \"All keyboard operable user interface must be operable with a keyboard alone.\" WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern requires the trigger to be a `<button>` element.\n\n**Why this matters**: A keyboard user pressing Tab will skip over every menu item. This is not a degraded experience \u2014 it is a complete wall. The navigation is invisible to them.\n\n**Fix**: Replace `<div className=\"menu-item\" onClick={...}>` with `<button className=\"menu-item\" onClick={...} aria-expanded={openMenu === idx} aria-haspopup=\"true\" aria-controls={`submenu-${idx}`}>`. Native `<button>` provides keyboard activation (Enter/Space), focusability, and correct role announcement for free.\n\n**Confidence**: HIGH\n\n---\n\n### 2. Submenu items are non-focusable `<div>` elements \u2014 no keyboard path to any destination\n\n**Evidence**: Lines 20\u201322. Each submenu item is a `<div>` with no `tabindex`, no `role`, no `href`, no keyboard handler.\n\n```jsx\n// Line 20-22\n<div key={cidx} className=\"submenu-item\">\n  {child}\n</div>\n```\n\n**User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users (complete access loss to all destinations), screen reader users (no link/button role, no navigable structure).\n\n**WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order). WAI-ARIA APG: navigation destinations must be `<a>` elements or `<button>` elements.\n\n**Why this matters**: Even if a keyboard user could somehow open the submenu (they can't, per Finding #1), they would have no way to reach any of the destination links. The entire menu is a dead end.\n\n**Fix**: Replace `<div className=\"submenu-item\">` with `<a href={child.href} className=\"submenu-item\">` (if destinations are URLs) or `<button>` (if they trigger actions). Wrap in `<ul>`/`<li>` for list semantics.\n\n**Confidence**: HIGH\n\n---\n\n### 3. No navigation landmark \u2014 screen reader users cannot identify this as navigation\n\n**Evidence**: Line 7. The outermost container is `<div className=\"megamenu-container\">`. No `<nav>` element, no `role=\"navigation\"`, no `aria-label` identifying the menu's purpose.\n\n```jsx\n// Line 7\n<div className=\"megamenu-container\">\n```\n\n**User group impacted**: Screen reader users (cannot use landmark navigation to find the menu), cognitive users (no structural context).\n\n**WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) \u2014 \"Information, structure, and relationships can be programmatically determined.\" WCAG 2.4.1 (Bypass Blocks) \u2014 landmarks enable bypass.\n\n**Why this matters**: Screen reader users navigate by landmarks (NVDA: N key, VoiceOver: VO+U). Without a `<nav>` landmark, this menu is invisible in the landmark list. The user cannot jump to it.\n\n**Fix**: Change line 7 to `<nav className=\"megamenu-container\" aria-label=\"Main navigation\">`.\n\n**Confidence**: HIGH\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n### 4. No `aria-expanded` state \u2014 screen reader users cannot tell if a menu is open or closed\n\n**Evidence**: Line 13. The toggle logic exists (`setOpenMenu(openMenu === idx ? null : idx)`) but no ARIA state is communicated. The `openMenu === idx` conditional on line 16 controls rendering, but no `aria-expanded` attribute is set on the trigger.\n\n```jsx\n// Line 13 \u2014 state changes but no ARIA\nonClick={() => setOpenMenu(openMenu === idx ? null : idx)}\n// Line 16 \u2014 conditional render, no aria-expanded\n{openMenu === idx && (\n```\n\n**User group impacted**: Screen reader users (cannot determine menu state), cognitive users (no programmatic indication of state).\n\n**WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value) \u2014 \"The current value of a user interface component can be programmatically determined.\" WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern: \"The button has aria-expanded set to true or false.\"\n\n**Why this matters**: A screen reader user hears \"Menu item, button\" but gets no indication whether the submenu is open. They cannot build a mental model of the menu structure.\n\n**Fix**: Add `aria-expanded={openMenu === idx ? 'true' : 'false'}` to the button element (Finding #1 fix).\n\n**Confidence**: HIGH\n\n---\n\n### 5. No `aria-haspopup` or `aria-controls` \u2014 screen reader users cannot discover the submenu relationship\n\n**Evidence**: Lines 10\u201314. No `aria-haspopup` on the trigger, no `aria-controls` referencing the submenu, no `id` on the submenu container (line 17).\n\n```jsx\n// Line 17 \u2014 submenu has no id\n<div className=\"submenu\">\n```\n\n**User group impacted**: Screen reader users (cannot understand the parent-child relationship), cognitive users (no structural cues).\n\n**WCAG/APG citation**: WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern: \"The button has aria-haspopup set to true. The button has aria-controls set to the id of the panel.\" WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships).\n\n**Why this matters**: Without `aria-controls`, a screen reader user cannot programmatically navigate from the trigger to its panel. The relationship between \"Electronics\" and its children is invisible to AT.\n\n**Fix**: Add `aria-haspopup=\"true\"` and `aria-controls={`submenu-${idx}`}` to the button. Add `id={`submenu-${idx}`}` to the submenu container.\n\n**Confidence**: HIGH\n\n---\n\n### 6. No keyboard event handling \u2014 Escape, Arrow keys, Home, End all absent\n\n**Evidence**: The entire component (lines 1\u201333) contains zero `onKeyDown` handlers. No Escape-to-close, no Arrow-key navigation between menu items, no Arrow-down to enter submenu, no Home/End for first/last item.\n\n**User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users (no alternative to mouse), screen reader users who use keyboard navigation.\n\n**WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), WCAG 2.1.2 (No Keyboard Trap). WAI-ARIA APG Menu Button pattern: \"Escape: Closes the menu and returns focus to the button. Arrow Down/Up: Moves focus between menu items.\"\n\n**Why this matters**: Even after fixing the `<button>` semantics (Finding #1), a keyboard user who opens a submenu has no way to close it (no Escape), no way to navigate between items (no Arrow keys), and no way to move between top-level menu items (no Left/Right arrows).\n\n**Fix**: Add `onKeyDown` handler to the menu container:\n- `Escape`: close open submenu, return focus to trigger\n- `ArrowDown`/`ArrowUp`: move focus between top-level menu items\n- `ArrowDown` (when on a trigger): open submenu, move focus to first child\n- `ArrowUp`/`ArrowDown` (within submenu): navigate children\n- `Home`/`End`: first/last item in current level\n- `Tab`: close submenu, move focus to next top-level item\n\n**Confidence**: HIGH\n\n---\n\n### 7. No list semantics \u2014 submenu items are not structured as a list\n\n**Evidence**: Lines 17\u201324. The submenu is a `<div>` containing `<div>` children. No `<ul>`, no `<li>`, no list structure.\n\n```jsx\n// Line 17-24\n<div className=\"submenu\">\n  {item.children &&\n    item.children.map((child, cidx) => (\n      <div key={cidx} className=\"submenu-item\">\n        {child}\n      </div>\n    ))}\n</div>\n```\n\n**User group impacted**: Screen reader users (items announced as generic text, not list items), cognitive users (no grouping structure).\n\n**WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) \u2014 \"Information, structure, and relationships can be programmatically determined.\"\n\n**Why this matters**: Screen readers announce list items with position (\"item 3 of 7\"). Without `<ul>`/`<li>`, each item is just anonymous text. The user cannot tell how many options exist or where they are in the sequence.\n\n**Fix**: Change to `<ul className=\"submenu\" id={`submenu-${idx}`} role=\"menu\">` with `<li role=\"none\">` wrapping `<a role=\"menuitem\">` or `<button role=\"menuitem\">`.\n\n**Confidence**: HIGH\n\n---\n\n### 8. No `aria-hidden` or `inert` on closed submenus \u2014 potential focus leakage\n\n**Evidence**: Lines 16\u201325. When `openMenu !== idx`, the submenu is not rendered (conditional render). This is actually acceptable for the *closed* state. However, when one menu is open and another is closed, there is no mechanism to prevent Tab from reaching hidden content if the conditional render is replaced with CSS hiding (a common refactor). More critically, the currently open submenu has no `aria-hidden=\"false\"` and closed ones have no `aria-hidden=\"true\"` if they were rendered but hidden via CSS.\n\n**User group impacted**: Keyboard users (Tab may reach hidden elements), screen reader users (may encounter hidden content in reading order).\n\n**WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n\n**Why this matters**: If this component is refactored to use CSS visibility (common for animation), hidden submenus will remain in the tab order. The conditional render pattern is fragile.\n\n**Fix**: If switching to CSS-based show/hide, add `aria-hidden=\"true\"` and `inert` to closed submenus. With conditional render (current approach), this is less critical but should be documented.\n\n**Confidence**: MEDIUM (current conditional render mitigates, but pattern is fragile)\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings (Friction but Workaround Exists)\n\n### 9. No `aria-label` on the navigation landmark\n\n**Evidence**: Line 7. Even after adding `<nav>`, there is no `aria-label` to distinguish this from other navigation elements (footer nav, breadcrumb nav).\n\n**WCAG citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n\n**Fix**: `<nav aria-label=\"Main navigation\">`\n\n**Confidence**: HIGH\n\n---\n\n### 10. Index-based keys instead of stable identifiers\n\n**Evidence**: Lines 11, 20. `key={idx}` and `key={cidx}` use array indices. If items are reordered or filtered, React will mismatch DOM nodes, potentially causing focus loss and ARIA state desync.\n\n**WCAG citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value) \u2014 state must remain associated with the correct element.\n\n**Fix**: Use stable identifiers: `key={item.id}` and `key={child.id}`.\n\n**Confidence**: MEDIUM (depends on whether items are ever reordered)\n\n---\n\n### 11. No `aria-current` for active page in navigation\n\n**Evidence**: No `aria-current` attribute anywhere in the component. If this menu includes the current page, it is not marked.\n\n**WCAG citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n\n**Fix**: Add `aria-current=\"page\"` to the link/button representing the current page.\n\n**Confidence**: HIGH\n\n---\n\n## Enhancements (Best Practice Not Met)\n\n- **No `prefers-reduced-motion` consideration**: If CSS adds transition/animation to submenu open/close, no media query is present. (WCAG 2.3.3, 2.2.2)\n- **No touch target sizing verification**: Without CSS, cannot confirm 24\u00d724px minimum targets. (WCAG 2.5.8)\n- **No skip link**: No mechanism to bypass this navigation to main content. (WCAG 2.4.1)\n- **No `lang` attribute context**: Cannot verify language marking on the page. (WCAG 3.1.1)\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (Gap Analysis)\n\n| Missing Element | Impact | WCAG |\n|----------------|--------|------|\n| `<nav>` landmark | Screen reader users cannot find menu via landmark nav | 1.3.1, 2.4.1 |\n| `<button>` semantics on triggers | Keyboard users cannot activate menu items | 2.1.1, 4.1.2 |\n| `<a>` or `<button>` on submenu items | Keyboard users cannot reach destinations | 2.1.1 |\n| `<ul>`/`<li>` list structure | Screen reader users lose list position context | 1.3.1 |\n| `aria-expanded` | Screen reader users cannot determine menu state | 4.1.2 |\n| `aria-haspopup` | Screen reader users cannot discover submenu existence | 4.1.2 |\n| `aria-controls` + `id` pairing | Screen reader users cannot navigate trigger\u2192panel | 1.3.1 |\n| `onKeyDown` handlers (Escape, Arrows, Home, End) | Keyboard users have no navigation model | 2.1.1, 2.1.2 |\n| Focus management (return to trigger on close) | Keyboard users lose context after interaction | 2.4.3 |\n| `aria-current=\"page\"` | Screen reader users cannot identify current location | 4.1.2 |\n| Skip link | Keyboard users must tab through entire menu | 2.4.1 |\n| `aria-label` on nav | Multiple navs indistinguishable | 4.1.2 |\n| Roving tabindex (if using menu pattern) | Tab order within menu is non-linear | 2.4.3 |\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n### Screen Reader User (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n**Experience**: This component is essentially invisible. No landmark to navigate to. No button role on triggers. No list structure. No state announcements. A screen reader user pressing \"N\" to cycle landmarks will not find this menu. Even if they find it by content, they hear a wall of anonymous text with no interactive structure. They cannot expand, navigate, or select any item.\n\n**Verdict**: Complete access failure.\n\n### Keyboard-Only User\n**Experience**: Pressing Tab skips over every element in this component. There is nothing to focus. The menu does not exist for them. No Enter/Space activation, no Escape to close, no Arrow keys to navigate. The component is a visual-only feature.\n\n**Verdict**: Complete access failure.\n\n### Low Vision User (200% zoom, high contrast)\n**Experience**: If the CSS provides adequate sizing and contrast, the visual layout may work. However, without focus indicators (elements aren't focusable), a user relying on keyboard + magnification cannot interact. In Windows High Contrast mode, the div-based structure provides no semantic hooks for forced-colors styling.\n\n**Verdict**: Partially functional visually, but interaction is blocked (same as keyboard).\n\n### Cognitive Accessibility User\n**Experience**: The visual structure may be clear (labels, indentation). However, without programmatic structure, a user relying on screen reader + cognitive support gets no context. No \"you are in navigation\" announcement. No \"item 3 of 5\" position. No state feedback (\"menu opened\", \"menu closed\").\n\n**Verdict**: Visually navigable for some, but no programmatic support for assistive workflows.\n\n### Vestibular & Motion\n**Alarm**: LOW \u2014 No animations visible in code. CSS may add transitions, but no `prefers-reduced-motion` is present.\n\n### Auditory Access\n**Alarm**: LOW \u2014 No audio elements in this component.\n\n### Environmental Contrast\n**Alarm**: MEDIUM \u2014 Cannot verify contrast without CSS. Flag for visual review.\n\n---\n\n## Perspective Alarm Table\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Zero semantic structure, zero ARIA, zero landmarks |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | Zero focusable elements, zero keyboard handlers |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | No focus indicators (elements not focusable) |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | No programmatic structure, no state feedback |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No visible animations in code |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | MEDIUM | Cannot verify without CSS |\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**REJECT** \u2014 This component fails at the most fundamental level of accessibility. It is not a case of \"80% of a pattern implemented\" or \"focus restoration missing.\" It is a case of **zero accessibility features present**. Every interactive element is a non-focusable div. There is no ARIA, no keyboard support, no semantic structure, no state communication, no landmark.\n\nFor an upgrade to **ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS**, the following must be addressed (in priority order):\n1. Replace all interactive `<div>`s with `<button>` (triggers) and `<a>` (destinations)\n2. Wrap in `<nav>` landmark with `aria-label`\n3. Add `aria-expanded`, `aria-haspopup`, `aria-controls` + `id` pairing\n4. Add `<ul>`/`<li>` list structure\n5. Implement `onKeyDown` for Escape, Arrow keys, Home/End\n6. Implement focus management (return to trigger on close)\n7. Add `aria-current=\"page\"` for active item\n\nFor an upgrade to **ACCEPT**, additionally:\n8. Add skip link\n9. Verify touch target sizing (\u226524\u00d724px)\n10. Add `prefers-reduced-motion` if CSS animations exist\n11. Verify contrast ratios in CSS\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (Unscored)\n\n- **CSS context**: The component references classes (`megamenu-container`, `menu-item`, `submenu`, `submenu-item`) but no CSS is provided. Contrast, focus indicators, touch target sizing, and reduced-motion behavior cannot be assessed. **Action**: Provide the CSS for a complete review.\n- **Item data shape**: The `items` prop shape is inferred (`{ label, children: string[] }`). If `children` items are objects with `href`, the fix is `<a>`. If they are actions, the fix is `<button>`. **Action**: Confirm the data contract.\n- **Multiple instances**: If this component renders multiple times (mobile + desktop), ARIA IDs (`submenu-${idx}`) will collide. **Action**: Confirm single-instance usage or add a unique prefix.\n- **Framework context**: Is this in a React app with a router? If so, submenu items should likely be `<Link>` components (Next.js) or `<NavLink>` (React Router) for `aria-current` support.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Remediation (Reference Implementation)\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';\n\nconst MegaMenu = ({ items = [] }) => {\n  const [openMenu, setOpenMenu] = useState(null);\n  const menuRef = useRef(null);\n  const triggerRefs = useRef([]);\n\n  const closeMenu = (returnFocus = true) => {\n    setOpenMenu(null);\n    if (returnFocus && openMenu !== null) {\n      triggerRefs.current[openMenu]?.focus();\n    }\n  };\n\n  const handleKeyDown = (e) => {\n    switch (e.key) {\n      case 'Escape':\n        closeMenu(true);\n        break;\n      case 'ArrowDown':\n      case 'ArrowUp': {\n        e.preventDefault();\n        const dir = e.key === 'ArrowDown' ? 1 : -1;\n        const current = openMenu ?? -1;\n        const next = (current + dir + items.length) % items.length;\n        setOpenMenu(next);\n        triggerRefs.current[next]?.focus();\n        break;\n      }\n      case 'ArrowRight': {\n        e.preventDefault();\n        const current = openMenu ?? -1;\n        const next = (current + 1) % items.length;\n        setOpenMenu(next);\n        triggerRefs.current[next]?.focus();\n        break;\n      }\n      case 'ArrowLeft': {\n        e.preventDefault();\n        const current = openMenu ?? -1;\n        const next = (current - 1 + items.length) % items.length;\n        setOpenMenu(next);\n        triggerRefs.current[next]?.focus();\n        break;\n      }\n      case 'Tab':\n        closeMenu(false);\n        break;\n    }\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <nav className=\"megamenu-container\" aria-label=\"Main navigation\" onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}>\n      <ul className=\"megamenu-items\" role=\"menubar\">\n        {items.map((item, idx) => (\n          <li key={item.id ?? idx} role=\"none\">\n            <button\n              ref={(el) => (triggerRefs.current[idx] = el)}\n              className=\"menu-item\"\n              aria-expanded={openMenu === idx}\n              aria-haspopup=\"true\"\n              aria-controls={`submenu-${item.id ?? idx}`}\n              onClick={() => setOpenMenu(openMenu === idx ? null : idx)}\n            >\n              {item.label}\n            </button>\n            {openMenu === idx && (\n              <ul className=\"submenu\" id={`submenu-${item.id ?? idx}`} role=\"menu\" aria-label={item.label}>\n                {item.children?.map((child, cidx) => (\n                  <li key={child.id ?? cidx} role=\"none\">\n                    <a\n                      href={child.href ?? '#'}\n                      className=\"submenu-item\"\n                      role=\"menuitem\"\n                      onClick={() => closeMenu(false)}\n                    >\n                      {child.label ?? child}\n                    </a>\n                  </li>\n                ))}\n              </ul>\n            )}\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n    </nav>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default MegaMenu;\n```\n\n> **Note**: This reference implementation addresses all CRITICAL and MAJOR findings. It uses native `<button>` and `<a>` elements (WCAG 2.1.1), `<nav>` landmark (WCAG 1.3.1), `aria-expanded`/`aria-haspopup`/`aria-controls` (WCAG 4.1.2, APG Disclosure), `<ul>`/`<li>` list semantics (WCAG 1.3.1), keyboard handlers for Escape/Arrows (WCAG 2.1.1, 2.1.2), and focus restoration (WCAG 2.4.3). Verify against your specific data contract and design system before adopting.",
